    {
      "id": "https://www.platformexecutive.com/journals/financial-services-journal/the-ai-revolution-in-banking-reshaping-the-economics-of-financial-services/",
      "url": "https://www.platformexecutive.com/journals/financial-services-journal/the-ai-revolution-in-banking-reshaping-the-economics-of-financial-services/",
      "title": "AI adoption boosts output but slows hiring, reshaping work",
      "content_html": "Anthropic said its 66 interviews with people at 58 U.S. companies using Claude found few domestic layoffs directly caused by automation, but many firms reported slower hiring and reduced use of contractors. Respondents said AI more often increased output than cut costs. The study was qualitative, and its customer sample was not randomly selected.\n\nA separate National Association for Business Economics survey found about one in five businesses reported widespread, measurable productivity gains. Roughly half reported some gains that were not widespread, while about 30% were not using AI or said it was too early to assess results. Austan Goolsbee, president of the Federal Reserve Bank of Chicago, said demand for construction and chips associated with AI investment is adding to current price pressures. He said the Fed might need to raise interest rates to curb inflation.\n\nFor 2025-30, Gartner forecasts AI will create 10.6 million jobs globally and eliminate 8.9 million, while more than 20% of workers—435.5 million people—will need to change their work habits. Anthropic’s interviewees described redesigning roles and moving staff to work that AI does not speed up as ways to benefit from the technology.",
