      "title": "Quebec court suspends province’s exemption from Crown oath",
      "content_html": "Quebec Superior Court Justice Ian Demers suspended the province’s 2022 exemption from the Crown oath required of lawmakers across Canada, saying Quebec could not change the constitutional requirement on its own. Premier Paul St-Pierre Plamondon says he will appeal.\n\nThe dispute could affect the legal standing of legislation passed by Quebec’s newly elected assembly, while the underlying challenge has yet to be heard on its merits. Ottawa lawyer Lawrence David, who brought the case, says the oath requirement applies to the King as Canada’s head of state. Laval University constitutional law professor Patrick Taillon argues that courts could temporarily preserve laws later found invalid, citing a Supreme Court remedy in a Manitoba language case. Quebec’s legislature is scheduled to resume Dec. 1.",
